course: dictionary
lesson: 10530
title: Tyre and its clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A tyre is the thick rubber ring around a wheel. You can often work out its meaning from the words around it. «The grooves in a tyre's tread push water away so the rubber can grip.» Grooves, grip and push water away are the clues: the tyre is something that holds onto the road.
explain: «A layer of water between tyres and road makes a car skid.» Here the word bald is not about hair. The clue is «worn-smooth "bald" tyres are dangerous in the rain.» Worn-smooth means the pattern has rubbed away, so bald must describe a tyre with no grip left.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If «tyre» is swapped for «wheel» in «A flat tyre is one with no air in it», it no longer makes sense, so the meaning of tyre must be the rubber ring itself. Remember that the American spelling is tire, but the meaning is the same.
fact: A tyre is a thick rubber ring, usually filled with air, that fits around a wheel of a car, bike or other vehicle.
fact: A flat tyre is one with no air in it.
fact: The tread is the pattern of grooves on a tyre, and it gives grip by pushing water away.
fact: «Bald» tyres are worn smooth, with little or no tread, and are dangerous in the rain.
fact: In American English the same word is spelt tire.
exercise: «The grooves in a tyre's tread push water away so the rubber can grip.» What do the grooves do? | Hold the air inside the wheel | Make the car heavier | *Push water away so the car can grip | Make the tyre louder on the road
exercise: «Worn-smooth "bald" tyres are dangerous in the rain.» What does bald mean for a tyre? | Filled with water | Covered in fur | Brand new and shiny | *Worn smooth, with little tread left
exercise: What is a flat tyre? | *A tyre with no air in it | A tyre that has been painted | A tyre with too much air in it | A tyre that is brand new
test: «Cold gas particles move slower and hit the walls less hard, so tyres have lower pressure in cold weather and drivers check them more often in winter.» Why do drivers check tyres more often in winter? | Because snow makes tyres bigger | Because tyres are louder in the cold | *Because tyres have lower pressure in cold weather | Because tyres are always flat in winter
test: «A layer of water between tyres and road makes a car skid; this is called aquaplaning.» What is aquaplaning? | A way to pump up tyres | A race of cars on the sea | *A car skidding on a layer of water | A kind of wheel with no tyre
exam: «A hard-topped metal car protects you because its metal body carries the lightning around you; the rubber tyres do not.» What protects you inside the car during a storm? | The rubber tyres | *The metal body of the car | The grooves in the tyres | The thin plastic seats
